function ch10() {
  return [
    h1("10. Conclusion and Future Scope"),
    h2("10.1 Achievements of the Project"),
    p("JustCmul8 set out to remove three barriers that keep Discrete Event Simulation away from most of the people who could use it: the price of commercial licences, the programming skill that open-source alternatives demand, and the installation that desktop software needs. What the project achieved is summarised below."),
    ...bullets([
      "**A complete no-code simulation platform:** twelve integrated modules, from creating an account and modelling on a canvas to analysis, sharing and collaboration, all running in a web browser.",
      "**A real engine and not an imitation:** each model is compiled into a genuine Python SimPy program and run by CPython compiled to WebAssembly in a Web Worker, so results come from the same library researchers use directly.",
      "**Verified correctness:** all 28 analytical checks against M/M/1, M/M/c and saturated-queue theory passed within tolerance, every deviation was below 2.1 per cent, and all 22 conservation and flow-balance invariants held on a multi-stage hospital-flow model.",
      "**Breadth of modelling:** fifteen node types across six application domains, including preemptive resources, typed stores, containers, channels, broadcasters, event synchronisers and interrupters.",
      "**Decision support and not only numbers:** bottleneck flagging, a Little’s Law consistency check, a composite health score, cost and return-on-investment analysis, and Monte Carlo confidence intervals with baseline-versus-scenario comparison.",
      "**AI-assisted workflow:** plain-English model generation and one-click, cost-aware fixes, served through a router that watches model health and fails over between models automatically.",
      "**Output that is easy to understand:** an eight-tab results dashboard, a WebGL digital-twin animation, an entity tracer and a traffic heatmap turn statistics into pictures.",
      "**Transparency and reproducibility:** a live Python code inspector and one-click Jupyter export let anyone verify a result independently.",
      "**Security built in:** Row-Level Security on every table, opaque revocable share links served through narrow database functions, secrets kept on the server and cascading deletion of user data.",
      "**Sustainable economics:** client-side computation and an open-source stack keep the running cost almost independent of usage.",
    ]),
    p("On the academic side, the project put material from across the programme to work and did not just cite it. Priority queues and graph traversal from data structures appear in the event scheduler and the validation pass. Normalisation, referential integrity and access control from database management appear in the schema and its policies. The software development life cycle shows in the scheduling decisions of Chapter 3, including the choice to tackle the riskiest component first. Probability and statistics appear in the distribution samplers, the confidence intervals and the check against queueing theory."),

    h2("10.2 Conclusion"),
    p("The decision that shaped the result most was our refusal to approximate the engine. Writing a rough event loop in JavaScript would have been far easier, and the platform would have looked much the same from outside. Instead, the graph is compiled into a genuine SimPy script, and the verification in Chapter 7 turns that from a claim into evidence: agreement with theory to within a few per cent shows that the generated script expresses the intended model and that the statistics are gathered correctly."),
    p("In practical terms the platform can be used today. A student can open a template and see queueing behaviour within a minute. A lecturer can demonstrate congestion live and send a share link afterwards. A small-business owner can describe a shop in plain English and read off a confidence interval before committing any money. All of this needs no licence, no installation and no code, which was the outcome we wanted. JustCmul8 shows that serious simulation can be as easy to reach as a web page, and that accessibility does not have to cost accuracy."),

    h2("10.3 Future Scope"),
    p("We designed the architecture to be extended: one registry defines each domain, the engine sits behind a worker boundary, and authorisation lives in the database. The roadmap below builds on those decisions and does not replace them, and it answers each limitation recorded in Section 1.4."),
    h3("Near-term enhancements"),
    ...bullets([
      "**Parallel Monte Carlo:** spreading replications over several Web Workers, so a hundred-replication analysis takes close to the machine’s core count times less, since the replications are independent.",
      "**Reproducible runs:** user-selectable random seeds, so any run can be repeated exactly and shared together with its seed.",
      "**Pause and resume:** checkpointing, so a long run can be suspended and continued later.",
      "**Streaming result aggregation:** dropping per-entity detail once it has been aggregated and keeping full traces only when playback is requested, which raises the ceiling on model size.",
      "**Role-based collaboration:** invitations by email with owner, editor and viewer permissions enforced by Row-Level Security, and version history with rollback.",
      "**Convergent conflict resolution:** replacing last-write-wins with a merge based on operational transformation or CRDTs, so two users can edit the same node without either losing work.",
      "**Input-validation hardening:** turning every boundary condition, such as a zero arrival rate, into a friendly message before a run.",
      "**Progressive runtime loading:** fetching a reduced Pyodide payload to shorten the first-run start-up.",
    ]),
    h3("Medium-term extensions"),
    ...bullets([
      "**Custom node types through a plugin interface,** run safely inside the existing sandbox, so users can model domains we did not foresee.",
      "**More simulation paradigms:** an agent-based engine and a true continuous solver next to SimPy, chosen per project.",
      "**Search-based optimisation:** sweeping a parameter space to find the configuration with the lowest cost that still meets a service-level constraint, so that advice becomes optimisation.",
      "**Offline and classroom mode:** a progressive web application with local storage for laboratories without dependable internet.",
      "**A native mobile companion** for viewing dashboards and playback.",
      "**A community template marketplace** where users publish and rate models for their own domains.",
    ]),
    h3("Longer-term directions"),
    ...bullets([
      "**Optional cloud execution** for models too big for browser memory, with client-side execution staying the default.",
      "**Parallel and distributed simulation:** splitting very large models across workers or machines with synchronised clocks.",
      "**Calibration from real data:** fitting arrival and service distributions automatically from historical or sensor data, so measured parameters replace estimated ones.",
      "**Live digital-twin integration:** linking a running model to IoT feeds so the simulation follows the physical system it represents.",
      "**Institutional features:** single sign-on, organisation management and classroom cohorts for department-scale adoption.",
    ]),
    p("None of these requires us to revisit what we decided in this project. The layered architecture, the worker boundary, the registry-driven definition of domains and node types and the enforcement of authorisation at the database were each picked to make extension possible, and every item above builds on them."),
  ];
}

